@@ PE1 | Agile
A new cross-functional team has been formed to build a mobile banking app. During the first sprint planning, it becomes clear that team members from marketing, IT and compliance each have a different idea of what the product is for. What should the scrum master do first?
A) Facilitate a vision session with the product owner and agree team working agreements
B) Assign backlog items by department so that each member can work in a familiar area
C) Start the sprint as planned and let a shared understanding emerge from the first increment
D) Ask the sponsor to send a written memo explaining the business goals to all team members
= A
? A shared vision and working agreements are the foundation of a new team. The product owner owns the vision, and the scrum master facilitates. Starting without alignment wastes a sprint, assigning by department undermines cross-functional work, and a memo is one-way communication that rarely creates shared understanding.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
At the kickoff of a new clinic construction project, the architects emphasise aesthetics, the engineers emphasise cost, and the client's representatives emphasise patient flow. What is the best way for the project manager to create a common direction?
A) Review the charter's objectives and success criteria with all parties to agree on priorities
B) Let each discipline optimise its own area and integrate the results at the design review
C) Ask the client to decide which group's priorities should dominate the overall design
D) Postpone the discussion until the detailed design is complete and trade-offs are clear
= A
? The charter's objectives and success criteria provide a shared reference for balancing competing priorities. Optimising in silos, forcing a single winner or postponing alignment all increase the risk of rework and conflict later.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
Halfway through a hybrid project, several team members ask why they are still building a reporting module after the scope was changed twice. Productivity is falling. What should the project manager do?
A) Revisit the vision and business objectives with the team and the product owner
B) Remind the team that the scope is approved and that questioning it slows everyone down
C) Ask the sponsor to attend the next stand-up and instruct the team to continue the work
D) Remove the reporting module from scope so that the team's motivation can recover
= A
? People need to understand why their work matters. Reconnecting the work with the vision and objectives restores purpose and may also reveal whether the module still adds value. Dismissing questions or using authority does not build shared understanding, and removing scope requires the decision-maker's involvement.

@@ PE1 | Agile
A product owner wants the team and key stakeholders to articulate, in a fun and concrete way, the three most important benefits that would make customers choose the new product. Which technique best fits this purpose?
A) Product box exercise
B) Pareto analysis
C) WBS dictionary review
D) Monte Carlo simulation
= A
? In a product box exercise, participants design the box the product would be sold in, forcing them to focus on its key benefits and creating a shared vision. The other techniques serve quality analysis, scope definition and quantitative risk analysis.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
The sponsor describes the project's goal as "making our customers happier." The project manager is drafting the charter. What should the project manager do?
A) Work with the sponsor to define measurable objectives and success criteria
B) Record the goal exactly as stated, since the sponsor is accountable for the business case
C) Define the success criteria alone, based on experience with similar projects in the past
D) Leave the success criteria out of the charter until the end of the planning phase
= A
? Charters should contain measurable objectives and success criteria (for example, a target customer satisfaction score). The PM collaborates with the sponsor to define them; leaving them vague or defining them alone creates misalignment.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
Following a merger, two teams from different companies must work together on a combined platform. Each team has its own tools, meeting habits and decision-making style. What should the project manager do to build a common way forward?
A) Facilitate a joint session to co-create a team charter with shared goals and norms
B) Adopt the practices of the larger team, since most people already know them well
C) Let each team keep its own practices and coordinate everything through the project manager
D) Ask the PMO to impose a standard methodology that both teams are required to follow
= A
? Co-creating a team charter builds ownership of shared goals and working norms. Imposing one team's practices or a PMO standard creates resistance, and keeping separate practices with the PM as a bottleneck prevents a unified team from forming.

@@ PE1 | Agile | multi
A distributed agile team works across four countries. The product owner is concerned that the team is losing sight of the product goal. Which two actions best help maintain a common vision? (Choose two.)
A) Connect each sprint goal explicitly to the product goal during sprint planning and review
B) Keep an up-to-date product roadmap visible to the whole team
C) Send the vision statement to the team once at kickoff and archive it
D) Allow each location to define its own goals to increase autonomy
E) Restrict the roadmap to managers to avoid confusing developers
= A, B
? Linking sprint goals to the product goal and keeping the roadmap transparent reinforce the vision continuously. A one-time message fades quickly, separate goals fragment the team, and hiding the roadmap undermines alignment.

@@ PE2 | Agile
During sprint planning, two senior developers have been debating for 30 minutes whether to adopt a new front-end framework. The rest of the team is disengaged. What should the scrum master do?
A) Suggest a timeboxed spike so that the team can make the decision based on evidence
B) Decide on the framework personally so that sprint planning can continue on time
C) Ask the product owner to choose the framework, since the product owner owns the product
D) Postpone the topic to the sprint retrospective so that planning is not delayed further
= A
? A spike turns an opinion-based debate into an evidence-based decision owned by the team, and keeps planning moving. Technical decisions belong to the developers, not the scrum master or product owner, and postponing leaves the conflict unresolved.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
A functional manager informs the project manager that she is pulling a key engineer off the project for two weeks to handle an operations emergency. The engineer is working on a critical-path activity. What should the project manager do first?
A) Meet the functional manager to understand the need and negotiate a solution
B) Refuse the request, pointing to the resource commitments documented in the charter
C) Escalate to the sponsor and ask for the functional manager's decision to be reversed
D) Accept the decision and update the schedule, since functional managers control resources
= A
? In a matrix environment, the project manager negotiates directly with the resource owner first, understanding both needs. Refusing or escalating immediately damages the relationship, and silently accepting ignores the impact on the critical path.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
Two days before a release, the test lead classifies a defect as critical, while the development lead insists it is minor. Both are frustrated. What should the project manager do?
A) Review the agreed severity definitions with both and involve the product owner
B) Side with the test lead, since quality must always take priority over the release date
C) Side with the development lead, since the developers understand the code best of all
D) Delay the release until the two leads manage to reach an agreement between themselves
= A
? Objective criteria (the agreed severity definitions) and the person accountable for business value (the product owner) provide a fair basis for resolution. Taking sides without criteria or delaying indefinitely does not resolve the conflict.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
On a construction site, the site supervisor wants to continue a crane lift in rising winds to stay on schedule, while the subcontractor's rigger says the wind speed is close to the safety limit. The disagreement is getting heated. What should the project manager do?
A) Stop the lift in line with the safety procedures until conditions are confirmed safe
B) Facilitate a discussion until both parties reach consensus on whether to continue
C) Let the supervisor decide, since the supervisor is accountable for the site schedule
D) Suggest a compromise in which the lift continues at a reduced speed with extra spotters
= A
? When safety is at risk, a directive (forcing) approach is appropriate: follow the safety procedures and stop the work. Collaboration and compromise are valuable in many conflicts, but not when people could be harmed.

@@ PE2 | Agile
During the last two retrospectives, two team members made sarcastic personal remarks about each other's work. Others have become reluctant to speak. What should the scrum master do first?
A) Speak privately with each member and remind them of the working agreements
B) Raise the issue openly in the next daily scrum so the whole team can discuss it together
C) Cancel retrospectives until the relationship between the two members has improved
D) Report both members to human resources for unprofessional behaviour in team meetings
= A
? Addressing personal conflict privately and early, using the team's own agreements, is the most respectful and effective first step. Public discussion can embarrass people, cancelling retrospectives removes an improvement mechanism, and HR involvement is for serious or persistent cases.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Two project managers need the company's only environmental test chamber during the same three weeks. Both projects have committed delivery dates. What should the project manager do first?
A) Meet the other project manager to explore scheduling options based on both projects' needs
B) Book the chamber immediately so that your project's committed dates are protected
C) Escalate to the portfolio manager and ask for a decision in favour of your project
D) Ask the sponsor to fund a second test chamber so that both projects can proceed
= A
? Peers should first try to resolve resource conflicts collaboratively — for example by splitting slots or resequencing work. If they cannot agree, escalation to the portfolio level is appropriate. Grabbing the resource or buying new equipment without analysis is not the best first step.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
One hour before a demo, two team leads must share a single test environment. Each needs it for the full hour. They agree to split the time equally, although neither gets everything they wanted. Which conflict resolution approach did they use?
A) Compromise
B) Collaboration
C) Smoothing
D) Forcing
= A
? Each party gave up something to reach a partial, acceptable solution under time pressure — the definition of compromise. Collaboration would have found a solution satisfying both needs fully.

@@ PE2 | Agile | multi
A developer repeatedly withdraws from technical discussions, and unresolved disagreements are now resurfacing as rework. Which two actions should the scrum master take? (Choose two.)
A) Talk privately with the developer to understand why they withdraw
B) Introduce practices that make it easier to contribute, such as sharing written proposals before meetings
C) Ask the developer in front of the team why they never contribute
D) Accept the withdrawal, since avoiding conflict keeps the team calm
E) Ask the developer's manager to replace them with someone more vocal
= A, B
? Understanding the cause privately and creating safer ways to contribute address the root cause. Public confrontation damages psychological safety, accepting withdrawal lets conflict fester, and replacement is not justified.

@@ PE3 | Agile
At the daily scrum, developers report that they have been waiting five days for the security department to approve a firewall change. They have tried emailing several times without a reply. What should the scrum master do?
A) Work with the security department to resolve the delay, escalating if needed
B) Ask the developers to find a workaround that avoids the need for the firewall change
C) Tell the developers to keep sending reminders until the security department responds
D) Remove the dependent stories from the sprint backlog and inform the product owner
= A
? Removing impediments the team cannot remove itself is a core servant-leadership responsibility. Workarounds that bypass security controls are inappropriate, and passively waiting or dropping work does not solve the problem.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A team member has missed three deadlines. In a private conversation, the project manager learns that she is struggling with a new modelling tool that was introduced last month. What should the project manager do next?
A) Arrange targeted training or mentoring and agree on a short-term improvement plan with her
B) Reassign her tasks to a more experienced team member so that deadlines are protected
C) Report the missed deadlines to her functional manager for her next performance review
D) Extend her deadlines so that she has more time to learn the tool on her own
= A
? The root cause is a skills gap, so training or mentoring addresses it directly while keeping the person engaged. Reassignment and reporting treat symptoms and can demotivate; extending deadlines alone leaves the gap in place.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A newly formed Scrum team asks the project manager to assign tasks to each developer every morning because "it is faster than discussing it". What should the project manager do?
A) Coach the team on self-organisation and help them choose their own tasks
B) Assign the tasks as requested, since the team itself has asked for this arrangement
C) Ask the product owner to assign the tasks, since the product owner sets the priorities
D) Let the most senior developer assign tasks, since they know everyone's strengths best
= A
? Self-organising teams decide how to do their work. Assigning tasks creates dependency on a single person and undermines ownership; the servant leader coaches the team to build this capability.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
After a release that caused a two-day outage, some team members start blaming each other in meetings. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a blameless review that focuses on systemic causes and improvement actions
B) Identify the person responsible for the outage and address the problem with them directly
C) Ask the team to stop discussing the outage and to focus on preparing the next release
D) Ask the sponsor to speak to the team about the importance of quality and accountability
= A
? A blameless review builds psychological safety and finds the system-level causes (processes, testing, tooling) that allowed the outage. Blame and silence both prevent learning.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
For six weeks, the team has worked long overtime to meet an aggressive milestone. Two people have reported stress-related illness. What should the project manager do?
A) Review the workload with the team and present options to the sponsor for a sustainable pace
B) Offer an overtime bonus so that the team stays motivated until the milestone is reached
C) Ask the functional managers to provide replacements for the people who are currently sick
D) Continue with the current plan, since the milestone has been committed in the contract
= A
? Sustained overtime harms people, quality and the project. The PM analyses the situation and offers the sponsor options (scope, schedule, resources), reflecting both accountable leadership and social sustainability.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A highly skilled senior developer, who is enthusiastic about the product, asks for the freedom to design a new module end to end. According to situational leadership, which style should the leader use?
A) Delegating
B) Directing
C) Coaching
D) Supporting with close supervision
= A
? High competence and high commitment call for a delegating style with low direction and low support. Directing or coaching would be inappropriate for a self-reliant expert.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
Three new members join a team that had been working smoothly for months. Soon there are more disagreements and decisions take longer. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate onboarding of the new members and a refresh of the team's working agreements
B) Remove the new members, since they have disrupted a team that was performing well
C) Let the team sort itself out, since experienced teams do not need any support
D) Make the decisions for the team until the new members have fully settled in
= A
? Changing membership often moves teams back to the storming stage. The leader helps the team re-form by onboarding new members and revisiting the team charter, rather than removing people or taking over decisions.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The scrum master notices that problems are only reported when they become critical. In private, a developer says people are afraid of looking incompetent. What should the scrum master do?
A) Model openness about mistakes and thank people who raise problems early
B) Require every developer to submit a written risk report at the end of each day
C) Tell the team that hiding problems will be reported to management from now on
D) Ask the product owner to monitor each developer's progress more closely each day
= A
? Psychological safety grows when leaders model vulnerability and reward early disclosure. Surveillance and threats increase fear and make problems even less visible.

@@ PE3 | Predictive | multi
The project manager wants to recognise the team's contributions effectively. Which two practices are most appropriate? (Choose two.)
A) Give recognition soon after the achievement, describing specifically what was done well
B) Adapt the form of recognition to individual and cultural preferences
C) Recognise the team only once, at the end of the project
D) Publicly reward a single "top performer" every week
E) Use monetary rewards as the only form of recognition
= A, B
? Effective recognition is timely, specific and appropriate to the person and culture. Delayed, win-lose or purely monetary recognition is less effective and can harm collaboration.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
During execution of a road upgrade, the team discovers that the regional transport authority must approve temporary road closures. The authority is not in the stakeholder register. What should the project manager do first?
A) Add the authority to the stakeholder register, analyse its requirements and engage it promptly
B) Continue with the planned closures and inform the authority on the day of the work
C) Ask the sponsor to obtain the approval, since the sponsor has the right contacts
D) Submit a change request to remove the road closures from the project scope
= A
? A newly identified stakeholder must be recorded, analysed and engaged. Ignoring a regulatory authority risks legal and schedule consequences, and changing scope is premature before understanding its requirements.

@@ PE4 | Agile
Key business users have not attended the last three sprint reviews, so the team is receiving little feedback. What should the project manager do?
A) Find out why they are absent and adapt the review format or timing to their needs
B) Cancel the sprint reviews until the users have more time available to attend them
C) Ask the product owner to accept all increments without waiting for user feedback
D) Report the users' absence to their managers and request that attendance be mandatory
= A
? Understanding why stakeholders disengage — timing, length, relevance — allows the PM to adapt engagement. Cancelling reviews or bypassing feedback weakens value delivery, and forcing attendance rarely produces useful engagement.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
Using the salience model, the project manager identifies a stakeholder with power, legitimacy and urgency in relation to the project. How should this stakeholder be treated?
A) As a definitive stakeholder requiring the highest priority
B) As a dormant stakeholder who can be monitored with minimal effort
C) As a dependent stakeholder who needs others to advocate for them
D) As a demanding stakeholder whose claims can usually be postponed
= A
? Stakeholders possessing all three salience attributes are definitive and deserve the most attention. Dormant stakeholders have only power; dependent stakeholders lack power; demanding stakeholders have only urgency.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A senior executive whose budget approval is needed for phase two is currently neutral about the project. The engagement plan states that the desired level is supportive. What is the best approach?
A) Meet the executive to understand their priorities and link the project to them
B) Copy the executive on all project emails so that awareness of the project increases
C) Ask the sponsor to instruct the executive to support the project's phase-two funding
D) Wait until phase-two planning starts before engaging the executive about the budget
= A
? Moving a stakeholder from neutral to supportive requires targeted engagement that connects the project to their interests. Flooding them with information or using authority does not build genuine support, and waiting leaves too little time.

@@ PE4 | Agile
A vice president regularly sends feature requests directly to developers, bypassing the product owner. The developers feel pressured to comply. What should the project manager do?
A) Meet the vice president and help channel requests through the product owner
B) Tell the developers to implement the requests quickly to keep the vice president satisfied
C) Ask the developers to ignore the requests, since they do not come from the product owner
D) Escalate the vice president's behaviour to the CEO as interference with the agile process
= A
? The PM engages the stakeholder respectfully and redirects the requests to the product owner, protecting the team's focus and the backlog's integrity. Complying bypasses prioritisation, ignoring the requests damages the relationship, and escalation is premature.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
The residents near a planned wind farm have started a social media campaign against the project, citing noise concerns. Construction starts in two months. What should the project manager do?
A) Meet community representatives to listen to their concerns and share mitigation plans
B) Proceed with construction as planned, since the permits have already been granted
C) Ask the legal department to issue a warning about potentially defamatory posts
D) Wait for the campaign to lose momentum before engaging with the local community
= A
? Early, respectful engagement with affected communities reduces opposition and may surface legitimate concerns. Relying solely on permits or legal threats tends to escalate conflict and reputational risk.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
A team member suggests publishing the full stakeholder engagement plan on the project's public wiki "for transparency". What should the project manager do?
A) Explain that the plan contains sensitive assessments and share a suitable summary instead
B) Publish it, since transparency is one of the core values of the project team
C) Publish it after removing the names of the project team members from the document
D) Delete the plan, since engagement strategies should not be written down at all
= A
? The engagement plan may contain sensitive notes about stakeholders' attitudes and strategies, so the PM uses judgment about its distribution. Sharing a suitable summary keeps transparency without harming relationships.

@@ PE4 | Agile | match
Match each power/interest grid position with the usual engagement approach.
1) High power, high interest
2) High power, low interest
3) Low power, high interest
4) Low power, low interest
A) Manage closely
B) Keep satisfied
C) Keep informed
D) Monitor
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Stakeholders with high power and interest are managed closely; high power but low interest are kept satisfied; low power but high interest are kept informed; and low power, low interest are monitored.

@@ PE5 | Agile
The sponsor expects every item in the release backlog to be delivered by a fixed launch date. The team's velocity data shows that only about 70% of the backlog can be completed by then. What should the project manager do?
A) Share the forecast and help the sponsor and product owner prioritise the launch scope
B) Commit to the full backlog and ask the team to increase velocity through overtime
C) Keep the forecast within the team for now to avoid alarming the sponsor unnecessarily
D) Move the launch date to match the forecast and inform the sponsor of the new date
= A
? Transparent, data-based forecasts let decision-makers align expectations: in adaptive work, a fixed date usually means flexible scope, prioritised by value. Hiding the data or overcommitting damages trust and sustainability.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
During user acceptance testing, the customer expects end-user training to be delivered by the project. The scope statement lists training as an exclusion. What should the project manager do first?
A) Review the scope statement and its exclusions with the customer to clarify what was agreed
B) Deliver the training anyway to maintain a good relationship with the customer
C) Tell the customer that training will not be provided and close the discussion
D) Submit a change request on the customer's behalf immediately to add the training
= A
? Revisiting the documented scope clarifies expectations first. If the customer still needs training, a change request can then be raised; delivering it informally creates scope creep.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
The marketing director wants the product launched with a set of promotional features, while the compliance director insists that launch must wait for full regulatory reporting. Both are influential. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a workshop with both directors using the business case and regulations
B) Choose the marketing features, since revenue is the main driver of the business case
C) Choose the regulatory reporting, since compliance always outranks marketing requests
D) Ask the team to deliver both sets of features by working extra shifts before launch
= A
? The PM facilitates alignment using objective references — the business case and mandatory requirements — and escalates to the sponsor if the directors cannot agree. Taking sides or overloading the team avoids the real alignment problem.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Several stakeholders believe the minimum viable product will be the complete product they will use for years. What should the project manager do?
A) Clarify the purpose of the MVP and show how the roadmap builds on it using feedback
B) Expand the MVP to include all requested features so that expectations are met
C) Avoid discussing the MVP's limitations until after the release to prevent concern
D) Rename the MVP as "version 1.0" so that stakeholders see it as a complete product
= A
? Expectations should be aligned early: an MVP is designed to validate assumptions with minimal investment, and the roadmap shows how it will evolve. Hiding limitations leads to disappointment and loss of trust.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
To avoid misunderstandings about quality at the end of a predictive project, what should the project manager do during planning?
A) Agree on acceptance criteria and quality metrics with the customer and document them
B) Ask the team to deliver the highest possible quality on every deliverable produced
C) Wait for the customer's reaction to the first deliverable before defining quality
D) Apply the organisation's default quality standards without consulting the customer
= A
? Explicit acceptance criteria and quality metrics align expectations before work starts. "Highest possible quality" invites gold plating, and waiting or ignoring the customer causes disputes.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
A senior executive tells the project manager that the new AI planning tools should allow the project to finish in half the planned time. What is the best response?
A) Discuss realistic expectations with data and propose a pilot to measure the impact
B) Agree, and re-baseline the schedule at half of its current duration straight away
C) Explain that AI tools cannot help with project schedules in any meaningful way
D) Ask the team to start using the tools and see what happens to the schedule
= A
? Aligning expectations requires honest, evidence-based discussion. A pilot provides data on real benefits. Blindly accepting or rejecting the claim are both poor choices.

@@ PE5 | Agile | multi
Which two practices best help align stakeholder expectations in an adaptive project? (Choose two.)
A) Frequent demonstrations of working increments
B) A transparent backlog and roadmap that stakeholders can see
C) A detailed fixed scope signed off at the start of the project
D) Reporting progress only at the end of each release
E) Avoiding showing unfinished work to stakeholders
= A, B
? Seeing working software regularly and having visibility of priorities keep expectations aligned with reality. A fixed scope contradicts adaptive delivery, and infrequent or filtered reporting creates surprises.

@@ PE6 | Agile
At a sprint review, the customer says the new search feature works but "is not what we imagined". What should happen next?
A) Add the feedback to the product backlog for the product owner to prioritise
B) Reject the feedback, since the feature met all of its documented acceptance criteria
C) Ask the team to rework the feature immediately within the current sprint
D) Escalate to the sponsor that the customer keeps changing the requirements
= A
? Feedback at reviews is valuable input for the backlog; the product owner decides its priority. The team should also improve story clarification (examples, prototypes) to reduce such gaps.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
Monitoring of stakeholder engagement shows that the sponsor's satisfaction has dropped, and the sponsor no longer reads the monthly reports. What should the project manager do?
A) Meet the sponsor to understand their information needs and adjust the approach
B) Send the monthly reports more frequently so that the sponsor stays better informed
C) Ask the PMO to remind the sponsor of the responsibilities described in the charter
D) Continue as planned, since the communications management plan has been approved
= A
? Monitoring engagement means acting on signs of dissatisfaction. A conversation reveals what the sponsor needs, and the plan is updated accordingly.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
The project manager learns that a key milestone will slip by three weeks because of a supplier problem. A meeting with the customer is scheduled in two weeks. What should the project manager do?
A) Inform the customer promptly, explaining the impact and the recovery options being considered
B) Wait for the scheduled meeting so that the delay can be presented in person
C) Wait until the recovery plan is approved so the customer hears a complete solution
D) Ask the supplier to inform the customer directly, since the supplier caused the delay
= A
? Bad news should be communicated early, with options. Waiting reduces the customer's ability to plan and erodes trust.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
The project manager discovers that the product owner has been promising stakeholders features that are not in the release plan. What should the project manager do?
A) Discuss the impact with the product owner and agree how to communicate the roadmap
B) Tell the stakeholders directly that the product owner's promises are not valid commitments
C) Add the promised features to the release plan so that the stakeholders remain satisfied
D) Ask the sponsor to replace the product owner with someone who follows the release plan
= A
? The PM supports the product owner in managing expectations consistently and transparently. Contradicting the product owner publicly or adding unplanned scope creates confusion and risk.

@@ PE6 | Agile
A stakeholder complains to the sponsor that the project is "too slow", without speaking to the project team first. The sponsor forwards the complaint to the project manager. What should the project manager do?
A) Meet the stakeholder, share progress data and agree how they will get updates
B) Ask the sponsor to tell the stakeholder that the team is working as hard as it can
C) Send the stakeholder the full backlog and velocity history without further discussion
D) Ignore the complaint, since the stakeholder did not use the agreed escalation channel
= A
? Direct engagement addresses the stakeholder's real concerns and expectations, and an agreed update mechanism prevents recurrence. Data alone, intermediaries or ignoring the complaint will not rebuild confidence.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
A customer is upset about the cost of a change request they submitted. What is the best way to manage the customer's expectations?
A) Explain the impact analysis openly and explore alternatives such as phasing or reduced scope
B) Reduce the estimate so that the change becomes more acceptable to the customer
C) Implement the change at no cost so that the customer relationship is preserved
D) Tell the customer that the cost is fixed and cannot be discussed any further
= A
? Transparency and options help the customer make an informed decision. Manipulating the estimate is dishonest, absorbing the cost creates uncontrolled scope, and refusing dialogue damages the relationship.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
After go-live, users report many minor issues and stakeholders are losing confidence in the new system. What should the project manager do?
A) Communicate a prioritised resolution plan and report progress regularly
B) Minimise the issues in communications to protect the project's reputation
C) Fix the issues quietly without informing stakeholders to avoid further concern
D) Tell stakeholders that minor issues are normal after go-live and need no action
= A
? Visible, prioritised action with regular updates rebuilds confidence. Downplaying or hiding issues damages trust.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
The lead engineer, who is the only person who understands the legacy integration, will retire in two months. What should the project manager do?
A) Pair the engineer with a successor now, combining job shadowing with documented procedures
B) Ask the engineer to write a complete manual during the final week before retirement
C) Hire a consultant after the engineer leaves to reverse-engineer the integration
D) Ask the engineer to remain available by phone for questions after retirement
= A
? Tacit knowledge is best transferred through interaction over time, supported by documentation. Last-minute manuals, later reverse-engineering or relying on informal favours are risky.

@@ PE7 | Agile
At the start of a new project, the project manager wants to avoid repeating the mistakes of similar past projects. What should the project manager do?
A) Review the lessons learned repository from similar past projects with the team
B) Wait for the first retrospective, when the team can identify problems from experience
C) Ask each team member to rely on their own experience from previous projects
D) Copy the plan of the most successful previous project and adapt it as needed
= A
? Organisational process assets such as the lessons learned repository should inform planning from the outset. Waiting, relying on memory or copying plans without analysis wastes available knowledge.

@@ PE7 | Hybrid
A new system will be handed over to the operations support team in three months. What is the most effective way to prepare them?
A) Involve the support team in the final iterations through shadowing and runbooks
B) Deliver a complete documentation package to the support team on the go-live date
C) Ask the support team to learn the system on the job after go-live has happened
D) Keep the project team available indefinitely so that it can support operations
= A
? Early involvement transfers knowledge gradually and builds the support team's confidence. A document dump at go-live or open-ended project support are ineffective.

@@ PE7 | Agile
Only one developer understands the payment module, and the team is blocked whenever that developer is absent. What should the scrum master encourage?
A) Pair or mob programming and rotating work on the module across developers
B) Assigning all payment work to that developer so that efficiency is maximised
C) Asking the developer to document the module in detail in their spare time
D) Hiring a second payment specialist so that there is always a backup available
= A
? Collaborative practices spread knowledge and reduce single points of failure. Concentrating work increases the risk, and documentation alone rarely transfers enough tacit knowledge.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
A vendor that built a key system will finish its contract next month. The internal team will maintain the system afterwards. What should the project manager do?
A) Ensure the contracted knowledge transfer activities are completed before final acceptance
B) Pay the final invoice and ask the vendor to send the system documentation later
C) Ask the internal team to learn the system by reading the source code themselves
D) Extend the vendor contract indefinitely so that there is no risk to operations
= A
? Knowledge transfer should be a contracted deliverable verified before final acceptance and payment. Deferring it or relying on self-study creates operational risk.

@@ PE7 | Agile | multi
An organisation runs six agile teams. Which two practices best spread lessons learned across the teams? (Choose two.)
A) A community of practice that meets regularly to share experiences
B) Cross-team sessions to share insights and experiments from retrospectives
C) Storing lessons learned in each project manager's personal folder
D) Sharing lessons only at the closure of each project
E) Keeping retrospective outcomes confidential within each team
= A, B
? Communities of practice and cross-team sharing spread knowledge continuously. Personal folders, end-of-project sharing and secrecy keep knowledge siloed.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
A project team of 9 people, including the project manager, adds 3 new members. How many additional communication channels are created?
A) 30
B) 3
C) 36
D) 66
= A
? With 9 people there are 9 × 8 / 2 = 36 channels; with 12 there are 12 × 11 / 2 = 66. The increase is 30.

@@ PE8 | Hybrid
Executives want a monthly view of a hybrid project in which the software team works in sprints and the hardware team follows a milestone plan. What should the project manager provide?
A) A concise dashboard of milestones, release progress, risks and decisions needed
B) The full sprint backlogs together with the detailed Gantt chart for the hardware
C) A separate report from each team, each prepared in its own preferred format
D) Verbal updates in the steering meeting only, to save report preparation time
= A
? Communications should be tailored to the audience: executives need an integrated, concise summary. Raw detail, fragmented reports or undocumented verbal updates do not meet their needs.

@@ PE8 | Agile
In a team distributed across three time zones, members outside headquarters keep missing decisions that are made in informal conversations at the office. What should the project manager do?
A) Record decisions in a shared channel visible to all and agree core overlap hours
B) Ask the remote members to relocate to headquarters to improve communication
C) Hold all discussions at headquarters times regardless of other time zones
D) Send a weekly summary email of the most important decisions to remote members
= A
? Transparent, written decision records and overlap hours make communication inclusive. A weekly summary is too slow, and relocation or headquarters-only scheduling exclude people.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
A stakeholder from another department asks the project manager for the detailed cost breakdown of the vendor contract, which is confidential. What should the project manager do?
A) Check who may release confidential information per the communications plan
B) Share the information, since stakeholders are entitled to full transparency
C) Refuse the request without explanation, since the data is confidential
D) Ask the vendor for permission to share the information with the stakeholder
= A
? The communications management plan specifies who may release confidential information. Following it balances transparency with contractual and legal obligations.

@@ PE8 | Agile
Developers complain that meetings consume nearly half of their week. What should the scrum master do?
A) Review each meeting's value with the team and remove or shorten meetings
B) Cancel all meetings except the daily scrum to give the developers more time
C) Ask the team to work longer hours to compensate for the time spent in meetings
D) Keep the meetings, since frequent communication is essential in agile teams
= A
? Communication should be effective, not excessive. Inspecting meeting value with the team — and timeboxing or using asynchronous updates — balances communication with productive time.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
Repeated misunderstandings with an offshore supplier are caused by language differences in technical discussions. What should the project manager do?
A) Use written summaries, visual aids and explicit confirmation of understanding after discussions
B) Speak more slowly in meetings and assume that the supplier understands
C) Communicate only by email so that verbal misunderstandings cannot occur
D) Replace the supplier with a local one to remove the language barrier entirely
= A
? Reducing noise in communication — through written confirmation, visuals and checking understanding — addresses language barriers. Assuming understanding or switching to email only does not solve the problem.

@@ PE8 | Hybrid
A major scope change will affect the workload of three departments. Which communication approach is most appropriate for explaining it?
A) An interactive session with the departments, followed by a written summary
B) An email to the department heads with the approved change request attached
C) A post on the project intranet page for interested people to read when convenient
D) An announcement in the next quarterly company newsletter to reach all staff
= A
? Complex changes that affect people's work need interactive communication to allow questions and build understanding, supported by a written record. Push or pull methods alone are insufficient.
